 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the direction field $v^*$ in addition to the DDF fields $\xi$ and $d$.
-*   **Strengths:** Leverages the efficient rendering and view-consistency of DDFs; avoids direct fitting of unoriented fields which can be unstable.
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to local minima during optimization; discontinuities at medial axes require careful handling [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The optimization step for $v^*$ is iterative and depends on the number of optimization steps and the resolution of the field.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. Depends on the storage of the direction field $v^*$.
+*   **Strengths:** Provides a robust UDF estimate from a DDF. Explicitly optimizing candidate directions (in variants) can be more stable than gradient-based methods [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Prone to local minima. Discontinuities at medial axes can complicate the direction field [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When a UDF is required from a DDF representation, especially when robustness to noise is needed.
+*   **When not to use:** When the medial axis structure is complex and discontinuities in $v^*$ are problematic for downstream applications.
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in the context of Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields for Ray-Based Shape Representations [S1]. It builds on the property that a DDF can induce a UDF if the direction field points to the closest surface point [S1].
+*   **Origin:** The method is described in "Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields for Ray-Based Shape Representations" [S1].
+*   **History:** It is part of the Viewshed Field (VF) learning pipeline, specifically for novel view generation and shape representation [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used in single-image 3D reconstruction (SI3DR) and scene composition [S1].
 
